#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097820 :tutorial/content "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dd2362a7-73d2-577f-aa31-2150f8f6b63f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097821 :tutorial/content "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ed29747-9007-50b8-9347-89e46eabc182" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2625 :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "584707ea-2c96-5389-926d-b9ac87adc17c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2626 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"}]}
